maya: quick heads-up — our SSE client on Windows is stalling under load. stream reader seems to stop for ~2-5s then emits a burst. repro on Node 18 and 20. logs:
```text
[stream] chunk recv: 120
[stream] chunk recv: 0
[stream] no data for 3500ms
[stream] chunk recv: 98
```
anyone seen buffer-starve / partial-json hang on Win? wondering if it's backpressure or parse loop blocking.

 tj: do you have the client read loop snippet? curious if you're parsing inline on the 'data' event.

 maya: simple node example (using http.request):
```js
const req = http.request(opts, res => {
  res.setEncoding('utf8');
  let buf = '';
  res.on('data', chunk => {
    buf += chunk;
    // naive: split on "\n" and JSON.parse per-line
    const lines = buf.split('\n');
    buf = lines.pop() || '';
    for (const l of lines) {
      try { handle(JSON.parse(l)); } catch(e) { /* ignore */ }
    }
  });
});
```

 oliver: that pattern is super common but on Windows the socket buffer + event loop scheduling can cause the data callback to pile up and block parsing. two quick suggestions:
1) parse only on nextTick/resume so 'data' doesn't do heavy CPU work inline.
2) treat SSE framing ("data: ") / ndjson as event-delimited, not per-newline JSON — chunks can split a single JSON across multiple 'data' events.

 emily: for SSE specifically, accumulate and look for the SSE event separator (\"\\n\\n\"). example:
```js
function handleChunk(chunk) {
  buf += chunk;
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buf.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1) {
    const event = buf.slice(0, idx);
    buf = buf.slice(idx + 2);
    const data = event.split('\n').filter(l => l.startsWith('data:')).map(l => l.slice(5)).join('');
    try { const obj = JSON.parse(data); onMessage(obj); } catch (e) { /* malformed/partial — drop or stash */ }
  }
}
```

 oliver: also consider using stream backpressure primitives: pause the response, process a batch of events, then resume. e.g.
```js
res.pause();
process.nextTick(() => {
  processBatch();
  res.resume();
});
```
That prevents the OS buffer from being overwhelmed and gives the parser breathing room. also setting a higher highWaterMark on the parser transform can help.

 tj: question — how do we handle transient 401/refresh while keeping SSE intact? reconnecting loses in-flight stream; do we resume from last event id or replay prompts?

 emily: best practice: SSE should include an event id you can checkpoint. on 401, refresh token then re-open the connection with Last-Event-ID header (or include resume cursor in query). make sure server supports idempotent resume. if not, fall back to reissue request and rebuild state.

 maya: great, i'll try converting to event-delimiter parser + pause/resume and add a small job queue so parsing happens off the hot 'data' handler. will also add Last-Event-ID handling for auth refresh. thanks :thumbsup:

 eventstream-bot: deploy v2.3.7 to staging includes SSE parser improvements and optional resume-token header.

 maya: perfect — i'll test on Windows VM and report back.

